//! GENERATED by `python -m lattice_rule_sdk.build` — do not edit.
//!
//! Every path this file cites resolves from the TEMPLATE it was rendered from,
//! `src/lattice_rule_sdk/carrier/lib.rs.template` in the `lattice-rule-sdk`
//! Python distribution — not from the generated crate, which lives in a build
//! directory and holds none of the files named below.
//!
//! The carrier: the Rust crate that turns a Python rule into the one thing the
//! engine's host will load, a core WebAssembly module that imports nothing
//! (`../../../../lattice/src/custom-rules/host.mjs`).
//!
//! ## Why a Rust crate is here at all
//!
//! Python has no toolchain that reaches core wasm with an empty import section.
//! Pyodide compiles CPython for the browser and expects a JavaScript host to be
//! there; componentize-py emits a component-model binary over WASI. The host
//! speaks neither — it hands a module no imports and refuses one that asks. So
//! the Python does not become the module: it is CARRIED by one. This crate
//! embeds an interpreter, the author's `rule.py`, and the SDK's `runtime.py`,
//! and implements the ABI's `lattice_evaluate` by running that Python.
//!
//! ## The three things this file does, and the order they must happen in
//!
//! 1. Register `runtime.py`'s namespace in `sys.modules` under the name
//!    `lattice_rule_sdk`, so the author's `import lattice_rule_sdk` resolves
//!    against a module object rather than a filesystem there is none of. It
//!    must happen first: `rule.py`'s import runs while `rule.py` is being
//!    executed, not when its `evaluate` is called.
//! 2. Execute `rule.py`.
//! 3. Convert the evidence into Python values, call
//!    `lattice_rule_sdk.drive(evaluate, bundle, needs)`, and convert the mapping
//!    it answers with back into a `Verdict`.
//!
//! Every one of those can fail, and each failure becomes an `unknown` verdict
//! naming the cause rather than an empty finding list — the invariant this
//! whole repository is judged against (`../../../../../AGENTS.md`).
//!
//! ## The object ceiling, which is the sharp edge of this design
//!
//! RustPython stores an object's strong reference count in the spare bits of one
//! `usize`, and on a 32-bit target that leaves **15 bits: 32,767**. Every live
//! Python object holds one strong reference to its own type object, so the
//! number of live dicts, of live strings, and of live `True`/`False` references
//! are each capped at that figure — and a rule's evidence is exactly a large
//! number of live dicts and strings. Past the cap `RefCount::inc` aborts, which
//! reaches the host as `lattice_evaluate trapped (unreachable)`: loud, but
//! naming nothing a reader can act on.
//!
//! So the conversion below spends a BUDGET, and refuses by name before the
//! abort can happen. Measured on this carrier before the budget existed, with
//! every kind converted: 4,774 projects, 3,486 edges, or 2,041 import records
//! were the largest that worked. `CONVERSION_BUDGET` sits under the smallest of
//! those in the unit that binds — total objects — so the refusal always arrives
//! first.
//!
//! Two things follow, and both are in `../../../README.md` rather than only here.
//! A workspace past the ceiling gets an `unknown` verdict naming its own size:
//! that is the honest answer, not a partial judgment over the records that fit.
//! And a rule that must judge a workspace larger than this belongs in the Rust
//! SDK, whose artifact holds no interpreter and has no such limit.
//!
//! ## What crosses the boundary, and what does not
//!
//! The evidence bundle is parsed on the RUST side, by the SDK crate's serde
//! model, and handed to Python as ordinary dicts and lists. The alternative —
//! passing the raw JSON text and parsing it in Python — was measured and
//! refused: the interpreter this carrier embeds has no `json` module, so it
//! would have needed the frozen Python standard library, which nearly doubles
//! the artifact (measured: 6.89 MB against 12.99 MB) and would not have moved
//! the ceiling above, because the object count is the same either way.
//!
//! Only the kinds the rule's own `needs` declares are converted. A rule that
//! reads a kind it did not declare gets a named refusal from the runtime rather
//! than an empty collection — the same refusal under the author's CPython,
//! because `drive` is told the same `needs` in both places.

